<BaseLayout title="The System — Skyhook Industries" description="How the Skyhook concept works: orbital rotation, high-altitude rendezvous, momentum exchange, electrodynamic reboost, tether structure and failure management.">
  <div class="shell">
    <section class="page-hero"><p class="eyebrow">The system</p><h1>A rotating orbital machine, not a tower to space.</h1><p>The entire structure remains in orbit. Rotation allows one end to move more slowly than the system's orbital center of mass during the lower part of its cycle, creating a rendezvous opportunity for a vehicle arriving from below.</p></section>
    <section class="page-section"><div class="callout"><OrbitalGraphic /><div><h2>Two motions at once.</h2><p>The system orbits Earth while the tether rotates around its own center of mass. Near the bottom of the rotation, tip velocity can oppose part of the system's orbital velocity. Near the top, it adds to it.</p><p>The exact orbit, rotation period, tip speed and rendezvous altitude are coupled design variables and are not yet frozen.</p></div></div></section>
    <section class="page-section prose"><p class="eyebrow">01 — Capture</p><h2>Rendezvous is a complete subsystem.</h2><p>A payload cannot simply fly near the tether and attach at leisure. Capture requires precise navigation, synchronized guidance, a realistic capture envelope, shock-load management, positive retention, abort logic and controlled release.</p><p>The old site described a specific 67 m spider-like grabber. That number is no longer treated as a requirement. The final capture architecture remains an open engineering trade.</p></section>
    <section class="page-section prose"><p class="eyebrow">02 — Momentum exchange</p><h2>Every payload changes the tether's orbit.</h2><p>When the tether accelerates a payload, conservation of momentum requires the tether system to lose orbital momentum and energy. A useful Skyhook therefore cannot be characterized only by the velocity it can give one payload; payload mass, tether mass, orbit, release geometry and mission cadence must close together.</p></section>
    <section class="page-section prose"><p class="eyebrow">03 — Reboost</p><h2>Recover the energy between missions.</h2><p>A conducting tether moving through Earth's magnetic field can exchange momentum through Lorentz forces. Electrodynamic reboost is attractive because it can use electrical power rather than conventional reaction mass.</p><p>That does not make the transportation system “fuel free.” The arriving vehicle still requires propulsion, the tether requires power and supporting spacecraft systems, and electrodynamic performance depends on orbit, plasma conditions, conductor geometry and field orientation.</p></section>
    <section class="page-section prose"><p class="eyebrow">04 — Structure</p><h2>A 4,000 km tether cannot be treated like one rope.</h2><p>The current direction is a tapered, redundant and sectional structure with multiple load paths. Local damage should not automatically propagate into loss of the full system.</p><p>Materials are a gating technology. Heritage polymer tether concepts are valuable reference points, but a few-hundred-tonne transportation-scale architecture would demand specific strength and long-duration environmental performance beyond what should be assumed without evidence.</p></section>
    <section class="page-section prose"><p class="eyebrow">05 — Failure management</p><h2>Survivability must be designed in.</h2><p>Micrometeoroids, orbital debris, fatigue, thermal cycling, atomic oxygen and deployment faults all matter. The architecture therefore separates three problems: damage tolerance, collision avoidance and controlled failure.</p><p>Deliberate isolation or severing mechanisms may ultimately be useful, but fragmentation is not automatically safe. Any such strategy must demonstrate predictable post-sever trajectories and lower total orbital and re-entry risk.</p></section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
